 life then  perhaps empower him to not be arrested  that night and then also perhaps  empowered him to not be convicted later  on that issue I think has kind of fallen  off of the main conversation that would  seem like a tough battle and it's  unclear you know what the status of  those are a few years removed what do  you think the status is of these kind of  Stand Your Ground laws and the fact that  they're still on the books in so many  states I think that you know the status  stays the same we saw it in the Jordan  Davis case where clearly Jordan was you  know just sitting in the vehicle and and  the individual who took Jordan life just  felt as though you know this you know  they're playing loud music let me just  shoot the car up so to speak and he had  no he really had no explanation why as  to why he did it  only he said he thought that they had a  gun in the car and so his defense was he  was going to try to you stand your  ground and and just just the notion that  this is the mindset of some individuals  that they can shoot and kill young black  and brown boys and without any witnesses  and and can get away with it just  thinking or knowing that they can get  away with it is certainly something that  we have to take a look at what would the  what would the what would it be  what would the momentum of that law be  if you had african-american men going  out shooting young white young men and  saying I was standing my ground I was  fearing for my life it would be a change  in that law so let's not wait until this  starts to happen to make a change to  this law let's change this law it's a  bad law and so we have to look at it  from from different different  perspectives discouraging and all that  that these laws haven't started to  change very discouraging it's very  discouraging just just to know that you  know these laws you know the law doesn't  work I mean how many I'm in how many  cases can you look at and say that this  was an african-american  that use